speechtext:
The Minister of Railways and I do not always agree, but to-night I find myself exactly in accord with the case as presented by him. The hon. member for East Lam'bton (Mr. Armstrong) may be actuated by the best motives, but I believe in all seriousness that his amendment would injure the very people whom he is hoping to help. As stated by the Minister of Railways, if the amendment moved by the hon. member were adopted, it would perhaps penalize more than any one else the men on the prairies who grow grain for

export and who require to import coal to keep themselves warm in the winter time. I would go a little further than the Minister of Railways who, in my judgment, has recently, as Acting Minister of Customs, done something to increase rates on the Great Lakes. I refer particularly to the removal of the coastal privileges, to reciprocity in shipping. Every restriction that we place upon vessels operating from one Canadian port to another enables the fewer vessels remaining operating upon the Great Lakes to charge the people just a little bit higher rates. If a fixed rate might be made that would be profitable during the summer months, it would be a ruinous rate in November because a vessel cannot be operated at the same cost in November as it can during the summer when conditions of navigation are practically ideal. Rates are affected also by variation in the carrying capacity of vessels. Some vessels are able to carry grain at a lower rate than others and some vessels are able to carry coal at a lower rate than others. The speed of vessels also has an effect upon rates. A vessel that can make the round trip between the head of the lakes and Montreal in seven or eight days can operate much cheaper than the vessel that takes two weeks. If this amendment should go through it would create conditions that the. Minister of Railways said would drive the business to Duluth. That was pointed out to our western friends at one time when they proposed to place certain restrictions on the inspection of grain at Fort William, and they discovered they were only driving the business over to Duluth away from Canadian trade routes. Therefore, I ask my western friends who are more particularly interested to look very carefully into this matter before giving their support to an amendment that will limit the number of vessels seeking cargoes at the head of the Great Lakes, and consequently enable the limited number of vessels to charge a higher rate. It will also discourage the building of new boats. If vessel owners charge rates which, as my hon. friend fears, may be fairly high, they will only be inviting competition and that competition will eventually regulate the rates. For these reasons I find myself in sympathy with the case as presented by the Minister of Railways, and I am compelled to vote against the amendment moved by the hon. member for East Lamb-ton. I believe that if that amendment prevails, it will restrict the carrying capacity of vessels on the Great Lakes, and;

will prevent tramp vessels from going up there seeking cargoes at perhaps a lower rate, whereas the lower rate, should it prevail, will eventually go back into the pockets of the men who grow the grain.
